  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three ways of encapsulating living bacteria in polymer-based materials. In all cases, the goal is to trap cells inside a protective structure while processing them into fibers or particles.\n\nOn the left, a wet-spinning setup extrudes a bacteria–PVA mixture into a bath under compressed air, producing a “living fibre.” A later step adds an outer protective layer, indicated as chemical vapor deposition or another shell coating. In the middle, a coaxial electrospinning method feeds two solutions through a concentric nozzle toward a collector, forming a core–shell nanofiber. The enlarged schematic shows bacteria confined in the inner core while the surrounding shell creates a separate outer barrier. On the right, a spray-drying or aerosol process starts from a polymer and bacterial dispersion, passes through a drying chamber and cyclone, and yields microgels or small capsules containing the cells. A related electrospinning-like step below also appears to generate compartmentalized fiber segments with bacteria enclosed in discrete domains.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights different fabrication strategies—wet spinning, coaxial electrospinning, and spray drying—for creating bacteria-laden fibers or microgels with protective encapsulation.",
